const ORDER = ['01', '02', '03', '04', '05', '06', '07', '08', '09', '10', '11', '12']

const CONVENTIONS = `
BINDING CONVENTIONS (every section must use exactly these names and rules so the 12 sections agree):
- Product: working name Zune (codename; name clearance pending). A kids-only custom Android 17 (AOSP android-17.0.0_r1 pinned) OS image + browser-based parent portal. Customer-owned Pixel 10a/9a phones are flashed and re-locked by the company in v1 (D15-D17, D21); own hardware is a later phase; NO skin on stock Android (D22).
- Components: ZuneGuardian (system app; Device Owner + supervision role; owns the single device channel, signed-policy enforcement, parent PIN, time engine); ZuneLauncher (gesture navigation, D31); ZuneSettings; ZuneSetup (first boot + QR pairing); ZuneUpdater (fork of GrapheneOS Updater); zune-station (flash/QA CLI) and the WebUSB installer; apps: Messenger, Calls (voice/video), Walkie, Assistant, Videos, Weather, Camera, Photos, Journal, Notebook, Reader, utilities. Backend: Zune Gateway, Policy Service, Comms Service, AI Gateway, Content Service, Weather Service; Zune Portal (web).
- ONE persistent device channel (TLS WebSocket) owned by ZuneGuardian carries signed policy, commands and signalling; media (voice/video/PTT audio) runs in the visible app because of Android 17 foreground-service rules. No Google push (no GMS).
- Hosting: India regions (AWS Mumbai ap-south-1 primary, Hyderabad ap-south-2 DR) via an Indian private limited (A11, unconfirmed). Indian NTP; 180-day logs.
- Age range 7-14 (D23) with bands 7-9, 10-12, 13-14. English only (D20). No cellular voice/SMS (D19); 112-only emergency path (D28). Wi-Fi + mobile data; Bluetooth on, NFC off, USB file transfer off (D31).
- Retention default: messages, AI transcripts and logs 12 months in a restricted vault (D27; subject to counsel); location only for parent-set places or SOS; no analytics/ad SDKs.
- AI: OpenAI default, Anthropic fallback (D29). Videos: Tier 1 licensed/open offline base; Tier 2 YouTube made-for-kids-only behind a kill switch (D25). WebView: Vanadium prebuilt, only Reader and Videos (D26). Captive-portal Wi-Fi unsupported in v1 (D30).
- Requirement IDs: prefix per section (OS-, LOCK-, REL-, BE-, COM-, AI-, CNT-, APP-, OPS-, CMP-, QA-, PRE-) + two digits; each is MUST or SHOULD, concrete and testable.
`

const BRIEF = `
PROJECT: Zune (see conventions below). Today is 2026-10-03.
AUDIENCE OF YOUR SECTION: a FRESH Claude Code session with NO memory of this conversation, which will BUILD and DEPLOY the system. It has: the repo (getplx/Zune, branch research/android-kids-foundation), AOSP network access (assume yes; it must verify first), a build host, two Pixel 10a/9a phones, and cloud accounts. Everything it needs to know and decide must be in the spec section you write (plus links to repo files). Write for an expert engineer who must act without asking the founder anything, except where you mark a gate.

READ FIRST, IN ORDER: ${REPO}/docs/REQUIREMENTS.md (AUTHORITATIVE dated decisions D1-D31 and assumptions; the LATEST decision wins; D4-D6 are superseded; D22 no skin; D23 age 7-14; D24 Guardian full control; D25 video tiers; D26 Vanadium WebView; D27 parent visibility/12-month retention; D28 112-only; D29 OpenAI default; D30 captive portal unsupported; D31 device defaults incl. gesture navigation) and ${REPO}/docs/HANDOFF.md (state, verified findings, conflicts). Then read COMPLETELY the research reports assigned to you (in ${R}/). Reports were written before many decisions: apply the decisions over the reports and list what you changed under "Reconciliations applied". Research evidence mostly came from GrapheneOS/LineageOS mirrors because Google's AOSP tree was unreachable: put every claim that was not read from Google's own android-17.0.0_r1 tree, and every [UNVERIFIED]/[INFERRED] claim, into your "Verify first" section with HOW to verify and WHAT TO DO IF FALSE.
${CONVENTIONS}
OUTPUT: write ONE markdown file: ${OUT}/<your file> (1,800-4,000 words). Structure exactly:
# <title>
## Purpose and scope  (Stage 1 vs Stage 2; what this section does NOT cover and which other section does, by filename)
## Decisions applied and reconciliations  (table: decision/report -> effect on this section; conflicts resolved)
## Requirements  (numbered IDs, MUST/SHOULD, concrete and testable)
## Design and build instructions  (components, repo paths under zune/, interfaces, schemas, commands, config values, sequences; code or pseudo-code blocks where they remove ambiguity)
## Acceptance criteria and tests  (observable checks)
## Verify first  (table: claim | why uncertain | how to verify | if false)
## Risks, open gates and out of scope  (mark gates as [GATE: before build | before staff pilot | before external family | before charging])
Rules: imperative and specific; no marketing; no filler; never invent facts, versions, URLs, prices or legal citations (mark unknowns); cite sources as [R04 F3] style; keep naming exactly per the conventions; do not contradict REQUIREMENTS.md. Do NOT modify any other file; do NOT run git.
`

phase('Write')
const written = await parallel(SECTIONS.map((s) => () => agent(
  `${BRIEF}\nYOUR SECTION: file ${s.file}; title "${s.title}".\nASSIGNED REPORTS (read fully): ${s.reports.map((n) => `${R}/${n}-*.md`).join(', ')}.\nSCOPE: ${s.focus}`,
  { label: `write:${s.file.slice(0, 2)}`, phase: 'Write', schema: { type: 'object', properties: { file: { type: 'string' }, words: { type: 'number' }, gates: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } }, verifyFirstCount: { type: 'number' }, crossSectionNeeds: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } } }, required: ['file', 'words', 'gates', 'verifyFirstCount', 'crossSectionNeeds'] } },
)))
const ok = written.filter(Boolean)
log(`${ok.length}/${SECTIONS.length} sections written`)

phase('Critic')
const critic = await agent(`${BRIEF.split('YOUR SECTION')[0]}
You are the CONSISTENCY CRITIC. ${ok.length} spec sections exist in ${OUT}/ (01-*.md .. 12-*.md). Read ALL of them completely plus ${REPO}/docs/REQUIREMENTS.md.
Find and FIX IN PLACE (Edit tool; you may edit any file in ${OUT}/ except none others): (1) contradictions between sections (names, numbers, retention periods, ports of responsibility, which component owns what, requirement ID collisions, milestone references, repo paths); (2) stale assumptions that violate REQUIREMENTS.md decisions D1-D31 (cellular calls/SMS, SMS vault, Hindi/Indic UI, US launch or COPPA-first framing, Anthropic-first AI, 10/25/100 cohorts, Snapdragon-first, hardware sales, skin on stock Android, YouTube-only video promise, three-button navigation); (3) missing coverage: anything a builder would need that no section specifies (e.g. secrets management, domain/TLS, backups/DR, observability, support tooling, legal copy, incident response, data migrations, app signing, versioning), and add it to the most appropriate section or list it; (4) unrealistic or unbuildable instructions; (5) claims stated as fact that the reports only support weakly: move them to that section's Verify-first table.
Then write ${OUT}/99-consistency-log.md: what you changed (file, before/after in one line each), unresolved conflicts needing a founder decision (ordered by impact), and the consolidated list of all [GATE: ...] items by gate type, and the consolidated Verify-first list ordered by what must be checked first. Return the structured result.`,
  { label: 'critic', phase: 'Critic', effort: 'high', schema: { type: 'object', properties: { changes: { type: 'number' }, unresolved: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } }, gateCounts: { type: 'object', additionalProperties: { type: 'number' } }, topVerifyFirst: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } } }, required: ['changes', 'unresolved', 'topVerifyFirst'] } },
)
return { written: ok, critic }
